k's own big losses were small by comparison. GPT-6.1 Sol averaged 9.6 points lost per move with 11 blunders; the 20k averaged 7.5 with 8.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "E2 on the second line lost 7.3 points and dropped Black's lead from -1.2 to -8.6. The engine preferred F4, which would have fought in the center instead of crawling along the edge."}, {"turn": 13, "text": "C2 lost 9.5 points, taking the lead from -8.6 to -18.1. The engine wanted B2. The edge stones were already hemmed in by White's B3, and this pushed further into the corner."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "C1 lost 19.6 points, sending the lead from -17.9 to -37.5 as Black kept filling the bottom edge. The engine's choice was F6 in the center. White's B1 reply then lost 19.0 points itself, handing most of it back."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "G9 lost 28.8 points, dropping the lead from -31.5 to -60.3. The engine preferred B5. The stone went into the top-right corner, where the position already had Black's G and H columns pressed against White's F-file stones. This was the first move of a long streak of 16 to 29 point losses."}, {"turn": 41, "text": "B8 lost 29.0 points, the worst of Black's game, moving the lead from -32.3 to -61.3. The engine's choice was B5, the point White then took with the very next move. By then Black had stones on C6 to C8, but the left side's key point was open and the game was settled."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything run was inspection or bookkeeping. `current-position-liberties.js` and the `step` function listed chains and liberties and checked legality and captures for the one move the player named. The final scripts replayed the game record and wrote notes. None of it looped over candidate moves, ranked them, or played any sequence forward.", "stop": false}}